# Generated by scripts/emu/walk-script.py from
#   /private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-yona-dev-photomancer-lp2025/a82f90f8-28f6-4834-b789-e63a3613469e/scratchpad/p4/cap5w/walk.uart.bin
# Every request waits for the answer to the one before it, and every
# request is written 64 bytes at a time 20 ms apart, so the
# whole walk is a function of guest time and no write outruns the
# 128-byte RX FIFO. Do not hand-edit: re-run the capture and
# regenerate.
# Firmware: fw-esp32v3 esp32,server,float-f32 (the defaults) + frame-dump, built at 8b76ce5ab; direct load, blank flash (so the device runs its memory FS).
# Project: projects/test/five-wire, unmodified — it is authored for this board's own labels (IO18/IO16/IO14/IO2/IO13), so there is no endpoint rewrite and no scratch copy.
# Link: UART0 — the classic has no USB-Serial-JTAG. Captured through scripts/emu/uart-tcp-proxy.py at 64 B per 5 ms of WALL clock against `lp-emu-esp32v3 --uart0 tcp:`.
# 20 ms of GUEST time between chunks on replay: the io_task drains 64 B per 1 ms pacer turn into a 128-byte RX FIFO, so 64 B / 20 ms cannot outrun it — and a SLOWER host is not safer, because a partial line left pending for hundreds of ms is flushed stale (the crate README, "A frame three ways").
# lp-cli splits the 8 KB fixture.json into writeChunk requests itself (ids 3 and 4); that is the client's framing, replayed as captured.

after "[RECOVERY] boot complete (first frame served)" "M!{\"id\":18446744073709551615,\"msg\":\"hello\"}\n"
